{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Only be strong not to eat the blood.</b> Since Israel was steeped in eating blood in the worship of demons, therefore the Torah needed to warn, ״לא תאכלו על הדם״ — and also here there was need to say 'be strong' and strengthen yourself against your inclination so that you subdue it and not eat it; and for this reason the Torah needed to mention its prohibition many times, because they were very accustomed to it and found it hard to refrain, all the more so since it brought a person to idol worship. And we find that the prohibition of blood is mentioned in the Torah seven times, and so is the way of the Torah with weighty matters, to mention them many times — like the matter of Shabbat, which is weighed against all the commandments combined, and it is mentioned in the Torah twelve times; and likewise the matter of the exodus from Egypt is mentioned in the Torah fifty times. Or one may say, 'only be strong' — since blood strengthens the body of the one who eats it, it was necessary to say the counterpart of this, that He will give him strength if he does not eat it. And the word רק comes to exclude the liver, for it is entirely blood; and likewise ״אך כאשר יאכל את הצבי״ — the word אך comes to exclude the fat, so that you not say: since it was likened to the deer and the antelope — just as the fat of the deer and antelope is permitted, so too here it is permitted. Rather, in that same respect in which one eats the deer and the antelope, concerning which there is no warning to eat it in purity, but like the deer and antelope that are disqualified for the altar and the impure and the pure eat from one bowl — so too one may eat it here; for if it had come to permit the fat, it would have said ״כל אשר יאכל בצבי ואיל יאכל בו״." "<b>For the blood, it is the soul.</b> This comes to give a reason for the prohibition of blood, and I have already elaborated on this in the section of אחרי מות." "<b>And you shall not eat the soul with the flesh.</b> Its sense is: and you shall not eat the flesh that was cut from the animal while its soul was still in it, even after the living one has died; and this is the prohibition of a limb from a living animal, which was said to the children of Noah in the verse (Genesis 9:4): ״אך בשר בנפשו דמו לא תאכלו״. And the reason is because it is a matter of cruelty and breeds a bad trait in the soul of one who is accustomed to it. And it says ״לא תאכלנו״, and this is a warning concerning the blood of the limbs."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:01.745466722Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}